"""Printed 183: incenter proof completion, Examples 4/5 and all Exercise 7.1."""
from math12_reviewed_unit1 import heading,paragraph,page
from math12_reviewed_higher_derivatives import eq,note
FIGURE={'type':'diagram','caption':'Figure 7.6','diagram':{'type':'source-incenter-length','title':'Figure 7.6 — Triangle LMN, its incenter O and perpendicular feet I, J and H; LO = 13, LH = 12, ON = 8'}}
QUESTIONS=[
'The three points are A(−1,3), B(2,1) and C(5,−1). Show that |AB|+|BC| = |AC|.',
'In each case, find the midpoint of the line segment PQ joining the two points P(x₁,y₁) and Q(x₂,y₂): (a) P(10,20), Q(−12,−8); (b) P(a,−b), Q(−a,b); (c) P(1/2,−1/4), Q(3/5,4/7).',
'In each case, find the coordinates of the point R(x,y) which divides the line segment PQ joining the two points: (a) P(1,2), Q(3,4) in the ratio 5:7; (b) P(3,4), Q(−6,2) in the ratio 3:−2; (c) P(−6,7), Q(5,−4) in the ratio 2/7:1.',
'In each case, in what ratio is the line segment PQ (joining the two points P(x₁,y₁) and Q(x₂,y₂)) divided by the point R(x,y): (a) P(8,10), Q(−12,6), R(−4/7,58/7); (b) P(−2,4), Q(3,6), R(4/5,3/5).',
'Find the centroid of the triangle ABC, whose vertices are the following: (a) A(4,−2), B(−2,4), C(5,5); (b) A(3,5), B(4,6), C(3,−1); (c) A(1,1), B(−2,−2), C(4,5).']
STEPS=[
['Use d(P,Q) = √[(x₂−x₁)²+(y₂−y₁)²].','For AB the displacement is (3,−2), so |AB| = √(9+4) = √13.','For BC the displacement is also (3,−2), so |BC| = √13.','For AC the displacement is (6,−4), so |AC| = √(36+16) = √52 = 2√13.','Therefore |AB|+|BC| = √13+√13 = 2√13 = |AC|.','B = (A+C)/2, so B lies between A and C; this also explains the distance equality.'],
['Use M = ((x₁+x₂)/2,(y₁+y₂)/2).','(a) x = (10−12)/2 = −1 and y = (20−8)/2 = 6; M = (−1,6).','(b) The opposite coordinates cancel: (a−a)/2 = 0 and (−b+b)/2 = 0, so M = (0,0).','(c) x = (1/2+3/5)/2 = (5/10+6/10)/2 = 11/20.','For (c), y = (−1/4+4/7)/2 = (−7/28+16/28)/2 = 9/56.','Thus (c) M = (11/20,9/56); doubling each midpoint and subtracting P recovers Q.'],
['Use the signed-weight formula R = (m₂P+m₁Q)/(m₁+m₂), with nonzero denominator.','(a) R = (7(1,2)+5(3,4))/12 = (22/12,34/12) = (11/6,17/6).','(b) The ratio 3:−2 has opposite signed weights; R = (−2(3,4)+3(−6,2))/(3−2) = (−24,−2).','For (b), R is beyond Q and R−P = 3(Q−P); do not apply the external-minus formula with a second negative sign.','(c) Multiply both entries of 2/7:1 by 7 to obtain 2:7.','R = (7(−6,7)+2(5,−4))/9 = ((−42+10)/9,(49−8)/9) = (−32/9,41/9).','Check (c): R = P+(2/9)(Q−P), placing it within the segment with PR:RQ = 2:7.'],
['A dividing point must have R = P+λ(Q−P) with the same λ from both coordinates.','(a) From x, λ = (−4/7−8)/(−12−8) = (−60/7)/(−20) = 3/7.','From y, λ = (58/7−10)/(6−10) = (−12/7)/(−4) = 3/7.','Thus PR:RQ = λ:(1−λ) = 3/7:4/7 = 3:4, internally.','(b) From x, λ = (4/5+2)/(3+2) = 14/25.','From y, λ = (3/5−4)/(6−4) = −17/10, which is different.','Therefore R(4/5,3/5) is not on line PQ and cannot divide this segment in any ratio. The exact printed coordinates are retained; no guessed replacement is used.'],
['The centroid is G = ((x₁+x₂+x₃)/3,(y₁+y₂+y₃)/3).','(a) x = (4−2+5)/3 = 7/3 and y = (−2+4+5)/3 = 7/3.','Therefore (a) G = (7/3,7/3).','(b) x = (3+4+3)/3 = 10/3 and y = (5+6−1)/3 = 10/3.','Therefore (b) G = (10/3,10/3).','(c) x = (1−2+4)/3 = 1 and y = (1−2+5)/3 = 4/3.','Therefore (c) G = (1,4/3); each result is the average of all three vertex vectors.']]
ANSWERS=['|AB| = |BC| = √13 and |AC| = 2√13.','(a) (−1,6); (b) (0,0); (c) (11/20,9/56).','(a) (11/6,17/6); (b) (−24,−2); (c) (−32/9,41/9).','(a) 3:4 internally; (b) no ratio exists for the printed point.','(a) (7/3,7/3); (b) (10/3,10/3); (c) (1,4/3).']
PAGES=[page('080_page_1.jpg',183,[paragraph('The coordinates of the centroid G(x,y) is obtained from equation (iii) by equating the x and y components:'),eq(r'G(x,y)=\left(\frac{ax_1+bx_2+cx_3}{a+b+c},\frac{ay_1+by_2+cy_3}{a+b+c}\right)','iv'),paragraph('Similarly, the internal bisector of the angle C also passes through the point G(x,y). Thus, the angle bisectors of a triangle ABC are concurrent and G(x,y) is the point of concurrency.'),note('The weighted point in this formula is the incenter, rather than the centroid named in the source.'),heading('Example 4'),paragraph('Muhammad Ayaan has a triangular piece of backyard where he wants to build a swimming pool. How can he find the largest circular pool that can be built there?'),heading('Solution'),paragraph('The largest possible circular pool would have the same size as the largest circle that can be inscribed in the triangular backyard. The largest circle that can be inscribed in a triangle is incircle. This can be determined by finding the point of concurrency of the angle bisectors of each corner of the backyard and then making a circle with this point as center and the shortest distance from this point to the boundary as radius.'),heading('Example 5'),paragraph('Find the length JO.'),FIGURE,heading('Solution'),paragraph('Here, O is the point of concurrency of the three angle bisectors of ΔLMN and therefore is the incenter. The incenter is equidistant from the sides of the triangle. That is, JO = HO = IO.'),paragraph('We have the measures of two sides of the right triangle ΔHOL, so it is possible to find the length of the third side. Use the Pythagorean Theorem to find the length HO.'),eq(r'HO=\sqrt{(LO)^2-(HL)^2}=\sqrt{13^2-12^2}=\sqrt{169-144}=\sqrt{25}=5'),paragraph('Since JO = HO, the length JO also equals 5 units.'),heading('Exercise 7.1')]+[paragraph(str(i)+'. '+q) for i,q in enumerate(QUESTIONS,1)]+[note('Question 4(b) gives a point outside line PQ: its x and y coordinates yield incompatible division parameters. Its printed question is preserved and the absence of a ratio is explained in the learning solution.')])]
PAGES[0].update(title='PLANE ANALYTIC GEOMETRY STRAIGHT LINE',visualStatus='Complete proof continuation, Examples 4/5 and all five exercise groups; native incenter figure',alternateVerification={'pdf':'Math Book for 12 class KPTBB.pdf','pdfPage':80,'side':'left','reason':'All source questions enlarged; fractional ratio 2/7:1 and non-collinear R(4/5,3/5) confirmed'})
EXAMPLES=[]
for n,q,steps,ans in [('4','Find the largest circular swimming pool that fits in a triangular backyard.',['A fitting circular pool must lie entirely inside the triangle.','Construct the internal angle bisectors of two corners.','Their intersection I is the incenter; the third internal bisector passes through it.','Drop a perpendicular from I to a side; its length r is the same distance to each side.','Draw the circle with center I and radius r; it is tangent to all three sides.','To verify it is largest, let Δ be the triangle area and s its semiperimeter. A contained disk of radius ρ has its center at distance at least ρ from every side, so Δ ≥ sρ. The incircle satisfies Δ = sr, hence ρ ≤ r.','For the ideal geometric model the incircle gives the largest radius; no numerical backyard dimensions are supplied.'],'The incircle, centered at the intersection of the internal angle bisectors.'),('5','Find JO in the source triangle LMN, with incenter O, LO = 13 and LH = 12.',['O is the common point of the three internal angle bisectors, hence the incenter.','The perpendicular distances OI, OJ and OH to the three sides are equal.','Triangle LHO is right-angled at H, with hypotenuse LO = 13 and leg LH = 12.','Apply LO² = LH²+HO².','Thus HO² = 13²−12² = 169−144 = 25.','Take the nonnegative length HO = 5.','Since JO = HO, JO = 5 units; the unused ON = 8 label is not needed.'],'5 units.')]:
 EXAMPLES.append({'num':n,'title':'Largest Circular Pool' if n=='4' else 'Incenter Distance','question':q,'steps':steps,'answer':ans,'progressiveSolution':True,'solutionOrigin':'SpaceBook learning enhancement','sourceReferences':[{'page':183,'jpg':'080_page_1.jpg','verification':'Source example and working visually checked'}],**({'sourceQuestionBlocks':[FIGURE]} if n=='5' else {})})
